      "writtenOn": "2020-05-29T16:57:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks like this is creating a relationship between config options in which TCPC-driven FRS is the default, and PPC-driven FRS is subordinate. I think it would make more sense to have a method-agnostic CONFIG_USB_PD_FRS (or similar), which would be set automatically based on CONFIG_USB_PD_FRS_TCPC or CONFIG_USB_PD_FRS_PPC, which would be what a board file would set. If possible, the code in the PE (or wherever) that enables or disables FRS would be method-agnostic. This might benefit from something like pd_frs_enable (or similar) in usb_common.c, somewhat analogous to pd_check_vbus_level.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d7d30a72_553e1f76",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1346479
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-30T16:40:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I like this direction.  I can also see where it is not exclusive as to TCPC or PPC.  Zork uses the board_tcpc_fast_role_swap_enable to adjust the GPIOs for the PPC. Making this change would allow me to hook my PPC driver and we could off the board_tcpc_fast_role_swap_enable.  The decision to call one of the functions could be based on drv-\u003eset_frs_enable being present in the respective tcpc/ppc structures.  At that point, I am not sure I see a need for the CONFIG_USB_PD_FAST_ROLE_SWAP_PPC.",
      "parentUuid": "a3e3e611_a342c162",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0e624451_cde589d5",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-01T18:08:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Without these flags though, we could have a situation where both the PPC and TCPC support FRS. This is the case on Volteer, the P8815 technically supports FRS timing but we are using the PPC. It is pretty easy to combine them if we decide that down the line.\n\nLet me make the change to shorten this config flag. I like your suggested names.",
      "parentUuid": "a3e3e611_a342c162",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b47fb087_05a65cf8",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-02T05:28:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Denis\u0027 proposal is even more aggressive about removing #ifdefs than I was imagining. If having the PPC and TCPC both react to the FRS signal is a valid configuration (I am unclear on this based on the preceding comments and brief doc reading), then checking pointers instead of using conditional compilation seems like a visually cleaner approach.\n\nRight now, the flags are used to control defining the function pointers in the PPC and TCPC generic code. If you want to enforce that only one FRS enable function is defined using this design, you can add preprocessor logic to config.h to enforce that at most one of CONFIG_USB_PD_FRS_{TCPC,PPC} is defined. You can find many examples of that sort of thing elsewhere in config.h.",
      "parentUuid": "0e624451_cde589d5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "98ae699f_2655995d",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-03T19:37:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This conversation is fragmented into the next patchset but I think it makes sense to call both the TCPC and PPC configuration, and have the decision to call be handled at the driver level based on the existence of the function. \n\nHere is the driver level, so I think that the board_tcpc_frs_enable should be moved out of this and moved to the specific ppc driver, we should be set up now to handle that. Now, this should only be called if the FRS_TCPC is set since this is directly writing TCPCI registers. I won\u0027t move it here so I don\u0027t break Zork.",
      "parentUuid": "b47fb087_05a65cf8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ff9ed578_fa5c56ad",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-04T16:13:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am confused by your last paragraph. Did you mean that we should move the call to board_tcpc_frs_enable to the specific TCPC driver?\n\nIs your last sentence saying that you think we should make the change previously described, but you don\u0027t want to do it in this CL? Or is it saying something about moving code to a certain location?\n\nWRT where to call the board function, it might make sense to move the call to the driver if only a few parts require some board-specific action to enable FRS, but usually board-specific code is called from a pretty generic location to make it easy for boards to define their own behaviors. This seems like a generic-enough location, unless we expect to need board-specific FRS-enable code for non-TCPCi TCPCs.\n\nIf you expect the board-specific action to be closely tied to a particular driver, e.g. TCPC foo will always need a certain GPIO to be set, then it might make sense to define a generic GPIO name and have the driver set that. Then, a board that uses that TCPC can just\n#define GPIO_TCPC_FOO_FRS_EN BOARD_NET_NAME\nin board.h.\n\nAlternatively, you could call something like board_set_frs_enable from within pd_set_frs_enable. A board could define it based on the values of the TCPC and PPC config options, or we could have something like CONFIG_USB_PD_FRS_GPIO if there is some truly non-PPC, non-TCPC way of doing this.",
      "parentUuid": "98ae699f_2655995d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "37b88e3e_e3db6a7e",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 640,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-04T16:29:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry this might be confusing. Not to the specific TCPC driver, this is the TCPCI driver so this should only be called if we are configuring the TCPC as the FRS trigger. So, this will not be defined and therefore never called, even though the tcpm_set_frs_enable is called, unless FRS_TCPC is set. \n\nThat way, if we have TCPC-specific things we need to do if the PPC is the FRS trigger, we can implement that in the specific TCPC driver and compile it based on the FRS_PPC define (even though it is the TCPC driver), and vice-versa for the PPC driver (as would be the case on Zork).\n\nRight now Zork is using that function (board_tcpc_fast_role_swap_enable) to set some PPC GPIOs as I understand it, I think Denis is saying this would better fit in the PPC driver instead. But for this CL, I think its out of scope to change the Zork FRS structure. Maybe we can open a bug to track that. I think the point is that the gpio setting isn\u0027t really a board-level configuration, it should be considered a PPC configuration. That said, I haven\u0027t really gone through the Zork implemenation.",
      "parentUuid": "ff9ed578_fa5c56ad",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 639,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 640,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "7b432baf345f1dddc7278ab4331e425ba1ee6c4c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
